From 993a6c84904e395db0506381cd58a57f2a9a96e1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: VPinInfinite <340424849+VPinInfinite@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] philipshue: DOF's PhilipsHueController and its
 auto-configurator, from DirectOutput

Ported from DirectOutput's PhilipsHueController.cs and PhilipsHueAutoConfigurator.cs
(Rambo3's, MIT) for Visual Pinball Infinite, under DOF's names, so a Cabinet.xml
written for DOF works unchanged: up to 50 lights on a bridge as 150 RGB outputs,
LedWizEquivalent 70 added by the auto-configurator for a controller the
Cabinet.xml names, commands paced to the bridge as DOF paces them (a light's
new colour no sooner than the measured delay after the last command, offs at
once, the delay remeasured every 3 s), every light it touched turned off at
the end.

DOF talks to the bridge through Q42.HueApi 3.21.0 (Michiel Post, MIT); in its
place LocalHueClient.cpp makes the same HTTP requests (description.xml and the
key's state for the connection check, PUT lights/<id>/state for a command),
writes the commands as Q42.HueApi writes them, and converts colours with its
HSB converter (Q42.HueApi.ColorConverters 3.20.1), down to .NET Framework's
TimeSpan rounding (a 100 ms transition is sent as 0, as DOF sends it).

Differences: commands go out in order from a thread of their own, where .NET
fires them in parallel; requests give up after 2 s, where .NET's HttpClient
waits 100 s; the commands left at the end are sent before DOF finishes (up to
3 s), as a process ending at once would drop them; the bridge's key is not
written to the log; the units' nested lock of DOF's CopyNewToCurrent, which
C#'s reentrant locks allow and std::mutex doesn't, is taken once.
